4. BOUNDED CALL STACK

4.1 Why a Stack

Plans can invoke sub-plans (PlanStep in A3 §2.2) up to D_invoke depth. The FSM must track which sub-plan is executing at each level.

4.2 The Stack

Definition 3 (Call Stack). The FSM maintains a call stack:

```
stack = ⟨ frame_0, frame_1, …, frame_d ⟩
```

where each frame is:

```
frame = ⟨ plan_id ∈ P, step_index ∈ ℕ, checkpoint_hash ∈ Hash ⟩
```

Properties:

· Bounded depth — d ≤ D_invoke (A3 §4).
· Bounded frame size — each field is finite.
· Finite stack — |stack| ≤ (D_invoke + 1) × |P| × K × |Hash|.

4.3 Stack Operations

Operation Trigger Effect
PUSH Entering a PlanStep Append a new frame
POP Plan completes Remove top frame, resume caller
PEEK Any frame-dependent decision Read top frame

Rule: PUSH when d = D_invoke transitions to ERROR_STACK. The FSM does not overflow.

4.4 Stack and Finiteness

Proposition. The reachable stack configurations are finite.

Proof. Each frame is drawn from a finite domain. Stack depth is bounded by D_invoke. The set of possible stacks is a finite union of finite products. □

The stack is part of s_ctx (A4 §2.1). It does not break the finiteness of S.

