7. FAILURE, HALT, AND REFUSAL STATES

7.1 The Distinction

· Refusal (REFUSE_*) — the agent declined to act, intentionally, within budget. This is a successful halt.
· Error (ERROR_*) — the agent encountered something that violated its declared bounds. This is a failed halt.

Both are terminal. Both log. Both proceed to POWER_DOWN.

7.2 Refusal States

State Trigger Error Code
REFUSE_GOAL No plan matches, or goal confidence too low 0x-AMBIG-GOAL
REFUSE_ACT VERIFY = FAIL with policy = Halt 0x-UNSAFE-ACT
REFUSE_BUDGET T-AET would be exceeded 0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED

7.3 Error States

State Trigger Error Code
ERROR_TIMEOUT Sub-task cycle budget exceeded 0x-TIMEOUT
ERROR_INVARIANT State left I (A4 §6) 0x-INVARIANT-VIOLATION
ERROR_STACK Call stack exceeded D_invoke 0x-STACK-OVERFLOW

7.4 Terminal Property

Proposition. Every path through the FSM reaches POWER_DOWN in finite steps.

Proof. By T-AET bound, the FSM cannot run more than T-AET cycles. Every state consumes at least 0 cycles and at most its declared cost. POWER_DOWN is reachable from every state via TASK_COMPLETE, REFUSE_, or ERROR_. Therefore every path terminates. □

