8. FORMAL PROPERTIES

The FSM establishes the following properties, which are the A1 contributions to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Property P1 (Determinism). δ_M is a function.

Proof. Every transition rule has a deterministic guard. Guards are decidable (A5). The rule set is finite. Therefore δ_M is a function. □

Property P2 (Totality). δ_M is defined for every (m, σ).

Proof. Every state has an outgoing rule for every guard outcome. This is checkable at ROM burn time by exhaustive enumeration of (m, guard) pairs. □

Property P3 (Boundedness). Every transition completes in bounded cycles.

Proof. Per-state costs are declared and finite. The hardware cycle counter enforces them. □

Property P4 (Termination). Every path terminates.

Proof. T-AET bound (Proposition 7.4). □

Property P5 (Invariant Preservation). δ_M preserves the invariant set I.

Proof. VERIFY checks invariants before COMMIT. If an invariant would be violated, the transition is refused. Invariant preservation is the A5 obligation, integrated here. □

These five properties are what the Verifiable Agency Theorem assumes. A1 is the architectural realization of those assumptions.

